  "question": {
    "text": "A conducting wire&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ab</mi></math> of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>l</mi></math>, resistance&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math> and mass&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> starts sliding down at&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> down a smooth vertical, thick pair of perfectly conducting rails as shown in the figure. A uniform magnetic field&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> exist in the space in a direction perpendicular to the plane of the rails. What will be the final constant speed of wire?<br />\r<br />\r<img src=\"assets\\qt0033_b5a077fa047d.jpeg\" />"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>If there are no dissipating forces then the system will oscillate but if there is some small resistance then the acceleration of the conductor is related to the net force as<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>v</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac></math><br />When the rod attains the steady constant speed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">t</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> is zero. Hence</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math></p>"
  }
